-- ownership: a recursive group returns a tuple of two lists and pushes onto
-- one of them before it calls itself. Inside the group, a call of a member
-- makes one fresh cell for all the allocations of its summary (§16.6), but
-- one annotation per result path for those with a container condition,
-- which names the arrays at its own place (#720). Taken for one cell, each
-- place's annotation carried the other places' conditions and put their
-- guards under itself, `[*]` at the tuple and `.0[*][*]` below the lists,
-- a level deeper every two rounds until the cut (§16.7): the summary held
-- guards at every depth down to it, and the group took 18 rounds (#754).
-- Now it settles in 3, with guards at `.0`, `.1` and their elements alone.
pub split : List Int, List Int, List Int → List Int × List Int
split items small large =
    case items of
        [] →
            ( small, large )

        [ first, …rest ] →
            if first < 10 then
                split rest (List.push small first) large
            else
                split rest small (List.push large first)
